{
  "_comment": "Segment maps for WinCtrl panels with a glass display. A cell is one character position; `segments` lists the absolute bit index in the device buffer for each of the cell's segment slots, in the order the glyph tables use. A glyph lists which slots it lights.",
  "_source": "SimAppPro app.asar: config/ScreenCharMapping/deviceScreenMemoryMapping.json and ScreenCharMapping.json, with each cell's startPos folded into its addresses the way mainsrc/LCDControl.js does at load time.",
  "_status": "CONFIRMED against hardware 2026-09-17 by capturing SimAppPro driving a CarrierAce UFC in a live Hornet mission. Replaying the captured SET_LCDS frames through this map rendered real cockpit content, the A/P page (ATTH HSEL BALT RALT CPL) and the COMM page (GRCV SQCH CPHR AM MENU), with 0 of 36 cells failing to match a glyph. Before that it had only been checked for self-consistency.",
  "_notes": [
    "A glyph is looked up by the WHOLE field value, not character by character. Multi-character keys such as ' 0' and '`0' are distinct glyphs, not the OR of their parts: '0' lights slots 0,5,6,7,9,13 while ' 0' lights 1,2,3,4,9,13, and '`' does not exist on its own. Observed live: DCS-BIOS reports UFC_COMM1_DISPLAY as ' 2', two characters for one cell, and the hardware was sent the ' 2' glyph. SimAppPro only falls back to OR-ing two glyphs when the pair is absent from the table.",
    "Writes go out in groups of `group_bytes` bytes at a time, so a change to one segment rewrites the whole group containing it. A cell can straddle two groups, which is why a capture shows cells passing through wrong letters between the two writes that settle them.",
    "Only 438 of the 768 bits are claimed by a cell. The rest are gaps in the driver's address space, not spare display.",
    "The wire frame is SET_LCDS (0x4c): `4c <group> <b0> <b1> <b2> <b3>`, len 6, addressed to part 0xbed0. Groups run 0 to 23. Capture-verified."
  ],
